  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/3db9a8b3774e6d05efa1d3cbe262a16e2824f249c9fecfebf605ec8de7ca0634.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{AB}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{EB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{EC}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DC}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{CB}$ in terms of vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{CB}=$    \n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $C$ to $B$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/1c1826cfb7602c6380fed1e2aa3c380f575a29185c46ca969d212d94f7c11aed.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CE}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{EB}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040990/assets/c013029af2f1d0dd727ac3c2dc701334fe51a9391b9691144a12f4cfff25c3d5.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EB} \\\\  & =-c+b \\\\  & =b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
